helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214				0	ast	[ReDim]Dim VarName [(start To end)] [As VarType][, VarName2 [(start To end)] [As VarType][,...]]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id711996				0	ast	Optionally, you can add the \<emph\>Preserve\</emph\> keyword as a parameter to preserve the contents of the array that is redimensioned.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148451				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	ast	\<emph\>VarName:\</emph\> Any variable or array name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149562				0	ast	\<emph\>Start, End:\</emph\> Numerical values or constants that define the number of elements (NumberElements=(end-start)+1) and the index range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155307				0	ast	Start and End can be numeric expressions if ReDim is used at the procedure level.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	ast	\<emph\>VarType:\</emph\> Keyword that declares the data type of a variable.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	ast	\<emph\>Keyword:\</emph\> Variable type				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728	11			0	ast	\<emph\>Bool: \</emph\>Boolean variable (True, False)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146121				0	ast	\<emph\>Date:\</emph\> Date variable				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	ast	\<emph\>Double:\</emph\> Double floating point variable (1.79769313486232x10E308 - 4.94065645841247x10E-324)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148616				0	ast	\<emph\>Integer:\</emph\> Integer variable (-32768 - 32767)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147348				0	ast	\<emph\>Long:\</emph\> Long integer variable (-2,147,483,648 - 2,147,483,647)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	ast	\<emph\>Object:\</emph\> Object variable (can only be subsequently defined by Set!)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729				0	ast	\<emph\>[Single]:\</emph\> Single floating-point variable (3.402823x10E38 - 1.401298x10E-45). If no key word is specified, a variable is defined as Single, unless a statement from DefBool to DefVar is used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148458				0	ast	\<emph\>String:\</emph\> String variable containing a maximum of 64,000 ASCII characters.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149581	19			0	ast	\<emph\>Variant: \</emph\>Variant variable type (can contain all types and is set by definition).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155601				0	ast	In $[officename] Basic, you do not need to declare variables explicitly. However, you need to declare an array before you can use them. You can declare a variable with the Dim statement, using commas to separate multiple declarations. To declare a variable type, enter a type-declaration character following the name or use a corresponding key word.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	ast	$[officename] Basic supports single or multi-dimensional arrays that are defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable if the program contains lists or tables that you want to edit. The advantage of arrays is that it is possible to address individual elements according to indexes, which can be formulated as numeric expressions or variables.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	ast	There are two ways to set the range of indices for arrays declared with the Dim statement:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153950				0	ast	DIM text(20) As String REM 21 elements numbered from 0 to 20				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146912				0	ast	DIM text(5 to 25) As String REM 21 elements numbered from 5 to 25				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153709				0	ast	DIM text$(-15 to 5) As String REM 21 elements (0 inclusive),				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150321				0	ast	rem numbered from -15 to 5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018				0	ast	Variable fields, regardless of type, can be made dynamic if they are dimensioned by ReDim at the procedure level in subroutines or functions. Normally, you can only set the range of an array once and you cannot modify it. Within a procedure, you can declare an array using the ReDim statement with numeric expressions to define the range of the field sizes.				20200411 15:09:53
