helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020204.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786				0	ast	end sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Using Procedures and Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	ast	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	ast	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	ast	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	ast	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	ast	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ast	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	ast	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	ast	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114				0	ast	Program code				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ast	END SUB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	ast	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	ast	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	ast	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397				0	ast	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	ast	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ast	Program code				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ast	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	ast	End Function				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	ast	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914				0	ast	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	ast	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276				0	ast	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765				0	ast	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ast	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	ast	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	ast	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982				0	ast	Scope of Variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814				0	ast	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186				0	ast	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208				0	ast	GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145258				0	ast	The variable is valid as long as the $[officename] session lasts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	ast	PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	ast	The variable is valid in all modules.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212				0	ast	PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	ast	The variable is only valid in this module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886				0	ast	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368				0	ast	The variable is only valid in this module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id5097506				0	ast	Example for private variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8738975				0	ast	Enforce private variables to be private across modules by setting CompatibilityMode(true).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id146488				0	ast	REM ***** Module1 *****				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2042298				0	ast	Private myText As String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2969756				0	ast	Sub initMyText				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id9475997				0	ast	myText = "Hello"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6933500				0	ast	print "in module1 : ", myText				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id631733				0	ast	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8234199				0	ast	REM ***** Module2 *****				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6969512				0	ast	'Option Explicit				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id1196935				0	ast	Sub demoBug				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id1423993				0	ast	CompatibilityMode( true )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6308786				0	ast	initMyText				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id4104129				0	ast	' Now returns empty string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id7906125				0	ast	' (or rises error for Option Explicit)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8055970				0	ast	print "Now in module2 : ", myText				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2806176				0	ast	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154368				0	ast	Saving Variable Content after Exiting a SUB or FUNCTION				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156288				0	ast	STATIC VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	ast	The variable retains its value until the next time the FUNCTION or SUB is entered. The declaration must exist inside a SUB or a FUNCTION.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155809				0	ast	Specifying the Return Value Type of a FUNCTION				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404				0	ast	As with variables, include a type-declaration character after the function name, or the type indicated by "As" and the corresponding key word at the end of the parameter list to define the type of the function's return value, for example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152899				0	ast	Function WordCount(WordText as String) \<emph\>as Integer\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Log Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149416				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Log function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149416				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03080202.xhp\" name=\"Log Function [Runtime]\"\>Log Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3145066				0	ast	Returns the natural logarithm of a number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159414				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	ast	Log (Number)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149457				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3150791				0	ast	Double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151211				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3151041				0	ast	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Any numeric expression that you want to calculate the natural logarithm for.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3150869				0	ast	The natural logarithm is the logarithm to the base e. Base e is a constant with an approximate value of 2.718282...				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	ast	You can calculate logarithms to any base (n) for any number (x) by dividing the natural logarithm of x by the natural logarithm of n, as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3145420				0	ast	Log n(x) = Log(x) / Log(n)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155131				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3152463				0	ast	Sub ExampleLogExp				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	ast	Dim a as Double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3151116				0	ast	Dim const b1=12.345e12				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	ast	Dim const b2=1.345e34				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3148616				0	ast	a=Exp( Log(b1)+Log(b2) )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	ast	MsgBox "" & a & chr(13) & (b1*b2) ,0,"Multiplication by logarithm function"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080202.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	ast	end sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	CDateToIso Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150620				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CdateToIso function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150620				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030107.xhp\" name=\"CDateToIso Function [Runtime]\"\>CDateToIso Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151097				0	ast	Returns the date in ISO format from a serial date number that is generated by the DateSerial or the DateValue function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159224				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149497				0	ast	CDateToIso(Number)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152347				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154422				0	ast	String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147303				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145136				0	ast	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Integer that contains the serial date number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147243				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156152				0	ast	Sub ExampleCDateToIso				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126				0	ast	MsgBox "" & CDateToIso(Now) ,64,"ISO Date"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030107.xhp	0	help	par_id3143228				0	ast	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Comparison Operators [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150682				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>comparison operators;%PRODUCTNAME Basic\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>operators;comparisons\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150682				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03110100.xhp\" name=\"Comparison Operators [Runtime]\"\>Comparison Operators [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	ast	Comparison operators compare two expressions. The result is returned as a Boolean expression that determines if the comparison is True (-1) or False (0).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147291				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149177				0	ast	Result = Expression1 { = | < | > | <= | >= } Expression2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145316				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147573				0	ast	\<emph\>Result:\</emph\> Boolean expression that specifies the result of the comparison (True, or False)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148686				0	ast	\<emph\>Expression1, Expression2:\</emph\> Any numeric values or strings that you want to compare.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147531				0	ast	Comparison operators				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	ast	= : Equal to				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154924				0	ast	< : Less than				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146795				0	ast	> : Greater than				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150541				0	ast	<= : Less than or equal to				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150400				0	ast	>= : Greater than or equal to				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	ast	<> : Not equal to				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154686				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153969				0	ast	Sub ExampleUnequal				20200411 15:09:53
