helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	File I/O Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156344				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020000.xhp\" name=\"File I/O Functions\"\>File I/O Functions\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153360				0	ast	Use File I/O functions to create and manage user-defined (data) files.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398				0	ast	You can use these functions to support the creation of "relative" files, so that you can save and reload certain records by specifying their record number. File I/O functions can also help you manage your files by providing you with information such as file size, current path settings, or the creation date of a file or a directory.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Display Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151384				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03010100.xhp\" name=\"Display Functions\"\>Display Functions\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149346				0	ast	This section describes Runtime functions used to output information to the screen display.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	InStr Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155934				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>InStr function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155934				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120401.xhp\" name=\"InStr Function [Runtime]\"\>InStr Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153990				0	ast	Returns the position of a string within another string.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3147303				0	ast	The Instr function returns the position at which the match was found. If the string was not found, the function returns 0.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3146957				0	ast	InStr ([Start As Long,] Text1 As String, Text2 As String[, Compare])				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148538				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763				0	ast	Integer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148473				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126	9			0	ast	\<emph\>Start: \</emph\>A numeric expression that marks the position in a string where the search for the specified substring starts. If you omit this parameter, the search starts at the first character of the string. The maximum allowed value is 65535.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3145609				0	ast	\<emph\>Text1:\</emph\> The string expression that you want to search.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3147559				0	ast	\<emph\>Text2:\</emph\> The string expression that you want to search for.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154758				0	ast	\<emph\>Compare:\</emph\> Optional numeric expression that defines the type of comparison. The value of this parameter can be 0 or 1. The default value of 1 specifies a text comparison that is not case-sensitive. The value of 0 specifies a binary comparison that is case-sensitive.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153361				0	ast	To avoid a run-time error, do not set the Compare parameter if the first return parameter is omitted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154366				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154217				0	ast	Sub ExamplePosition				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	ast	Dim sInput As String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042				0	ast	Dim iPos as Integer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3144760				0	ast	sInput = "Office"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154125				0	ast	iPos = Instr(sInput,"c")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3145173				0	ast	print iPos				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	ast	end sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Basics				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	bm_id4488967				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>fundamentals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subroutines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;global and local\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modules;subroutines and functions\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154927				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01010210.xhp\" name=\"Basics\"\>Basics\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156023				0	ast	This section provides the fundamentals for working with $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3147560				0	ast	$[officename] Basic code is based on subroutines and functions that are specified between \<emph\>sub...end sub\</emph\> and \<emph\>function...end function\</emph\> sections. Each Sub or Function can call other Subs and Functions. If you take care to write generic code for a Sub or Function, you can probably re-use it in other programs. See also \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\" name=\"Procedures and Functions\"\>Procedures and Functions\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	ast	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	ast	What is a Sub?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	ast	\<emph\>Sub\</emph\> is the short form of \<emph\>subroutine\</emph\>, that is used to handle a certain task within a program. Subs are used to split a task into individual procedures. Splitting a program into procedures and sub-procedures enhances readability and reduces the error-proneness. A sub possibly takes some arguments as parameters but does not return any values back to the calling sub or function, for example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	ast	DoSomethingWithTheValues(MyFirstValue,MySecondValue)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156282				0	ast	What is a Function?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424				0	ast	A \<emph\>function\</emph\> is essentially a sub, which returns a value. You may use a function at the right side of a variable declaration, or at other places where you normally use values, for example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	ast	MySecondValue = myFunction(MyFirstValue)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153364				0	ast	Global and local variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	ast	Global variables are valid for all subs and functions inside a module. They are declared at the beginning of a module before the first sub or function starts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012				0	ast	Variables that you declare within a sub or function are valid only inside this sub or function. These variables override global variables with the same name and local variables with the same name coming from superordinate subs or functions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150010				0	ast	Structuring				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	ast	After separating your program into procedures and functions (Subs and Functions), you can save these procedures and functions as files for reuse in other projects. $[officename] Basic supports \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Modules and Libraries\"\>Modules and Libraries\</link\>. Subs and functions are always contained in modules. You can define modules to be global or part of a document. Multiple modules can be combined to a library.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	ast	You can copy or move subs, functions, modules and libraries from one file to another by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01/06130000.xhp\" name=\"Macro\"\>Macro\</link\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Tan Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148550				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Tan function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148550				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03080104.xhp\" name=\"Tan Function [Runtime]\"\>Tan Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3148663				0	ast	Determines the tangent of an angle. The angle is specified in radians.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	ast	Using the angle Alpha, the Tan Function calculates the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the side adjacent to the angle in a right-angled triangle.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	ast	Tan(Alpha) = side opposite the angle/side adjacent to angle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145174				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042				0	ast	Tan (Number)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156214				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	ast	Double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155132				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	ast	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Any numeric expression that you want to calculate the tangent for (in radians).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	ast	To convert degrees to radians, multiply by Pi/180. To convert radians to degrees, multiply by 180/Pi.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	ast	degrees=(radiant*180)/Pi				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ast	radiant=(degrees*Pi)/180				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	ast	Pi is approximately 3.141593.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149483				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3148646				0	ast	REM In this example, the following entry is possible for a right-angled triangle:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	ast	REM The side opposite the angle and the angle (in degrees) to calculate the length of the side adjacent to the angle:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3151115				0	ast	Sub ExampleTangens				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	ast	REM Pi = 3.1415926 is a pre-defined variable				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3145800				0	ast	Dim d1 as Double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3150417				0	ast	Dim dAlpha as Double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	ast	d1 = InputBox$ ("Enter the length of the side opposite the angle: ","opposite")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3149582				0	ast	dAlpha = InputBox$ ("Enter the Alpha angle (in degrees): ","Alpha")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3154016				0	ast	Print "the length of the side adjacent the angle is"; (d1 / tan (dAlpha * Pi / 180))				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080104.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	ast	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Seek Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154367				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Seek function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154367				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020304.xhp\" name=\"Seek Function [Runtime]\"\>Seek Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	ast	Returns the position for the next writing or reading in a file that was opened with the open statement.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3153194				0	ast	For random access files, the Seek function returns the number of the next record to be read.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831				0	ast	For all other files, the function returns the byte position at which the next operation is to occur.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	ast	See also: \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020103.xhp\" name=\"Open\"\>Open\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020305.xhp\" name=\"Seek\"\>Seek\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152460				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3145365				0	ast	Seek (FileNumber)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	ast	Long				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149665				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020304.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645				0	ast	\<emph\>FileNumber:\</emph\> The data channel number used in the Open statement.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Loc Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Loc function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020302.xhp\" name=\"Loc Function [Runtime]\"\>Loc Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3154138				0	ast	Returns the current position in an open file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156422				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3150768				0	ast	Loc(FileNumber)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150440				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	ast	Long				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152462				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	ast	\<emph\>FileNumber:\</emph\> Any numeric expression that contains the file number that is set by the Open statement for the respective file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	ast	If the Loc function is used for an open random access file, it returns the number of the last record that was last read or written.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020302.xhp	0	help	par_id3151115				0	ast	For a sequential file, the Loc function returns the position in a file divided by 128. For binary files, the position of the last read or written byte is returned.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Watch Window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149457				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050100.xhp\"\>Watch Window\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154908				0	ast	The Watch window allows you to observe the value of variables during the execution of a program. Define the variable in the Watch text box. Click on \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/02/11080000.xhp\"\>Enable Watch\</link\> to add the variable to the list box and to display its values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145173				0	ast	Watch				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155132				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_BASICIDE_WATCHWINDOW_EDIT\"\>Enter the name of the variable whose value is to be monitored.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148645				0	ast	Remove Watch				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01050100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_BASICIDE_REMOVEWATCH\"\>Removes the selected variable from the list of watched variables.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
