helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Eqv Operator [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156344				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Eqv operator (logical)\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156344				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03060200.xhp\" name=\"Eqv Operator [Runtime]\"\>Eqv Operator [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149656				0	ast	Calculates the logical equivalence of two expressions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154367				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154910				0	ast	Result = Expression1 Eqv Expression2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151043				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150869				0	ast	\<emph\>Result:\</emph\> Any numeric variable that contains the result of the comparison.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150448				0	ast	\<emph\>Expression1, Expression2:\</emph\> Any expressions that you want to compare.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149562				0	ast	When testing for equivalence between Boolean expressions, the result is \<emph\>True\</emph\> if both expressions are either \<emph\>True\</emph\> or \<emph\>False\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	ast	In a bit-wise comparison, the Eqv operator only sets the corresponding bit in the result if a bit is set in both expressions, or in neither expression.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159154				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	ast	Sub ExampleEqv				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155308				0	ast	Dim A as Variant, B as Variant, C as Variant, D as Variant				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	ast	Dim vOut as Variant				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	ast	A = 10: B = 8: C = 6: D = Null				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152462				0	ast	vOut = A > B Eqv B > C REM returns -1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153191				0	ast	vOut = B > A Eqv B > C REM returns 0				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ast	vOut = A > B Eqv B > D REM returns 0				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	ast	vOut = (B > D Eqv B > A) REM returns -1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149959				0	ast	vOut = B Eqv A REM returns -3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145646				0	ast	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Format Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153539				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Format function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153539				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120301.xhp\" name=\"Format Function [Runtime]\"\>Format Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	ast	Converts a number to a string, and then formats it according to the format that you specify.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	ast	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	ast	Format (Number [, Format As String])				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149178				0	ast	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148474				0	ast	String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176				0	ast	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	ast	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531				0	ast	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561				0	ast	Formatting Codes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	ast	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	ast	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	ast	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	ast	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	ast	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	ast	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150				0	ast	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	ast	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	ast	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481				0	ast	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	ast	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617				0	ast	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713				0	ast	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887				0	ast	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	ast	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139				0	ast	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366				0	ast	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	ast	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	ast	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113				0	ast	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	ast	Predefined format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	ast	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	ast	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	ast	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	ast	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	ast	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	ast	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	ast	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149019				0	ast	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054				0	ast	Sub ExampleFormat				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	ast	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	ast	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	ast	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				20200411 15:09:53
