helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\measurement_units.xhp	0	help	par_id3148473				0	ast	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01040200.xhp\" name=\"Writer - General\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Writer - General\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Integrating new UNO components				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149760				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>add-ons, see UNO components\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>UNO components;integrating new\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>installing;UNO components\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149760				0	ast	\<variable id=\"integratinguno\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/integratinguno.xhp\" name=\"Integrating new UNO components\"\>Integrating new UNO components\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571				0	ast	Programmers can write and integrate their own UNO (Universal Network Objects) components to $[officename]. Those new components can be added to the $[officename] menus and toolbars; we call them "Add-Ons".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3154751				0	ast	The integration of new components is supported by some tools and services. Details can be found in the $[officename] Developer's Guide. The three main steps are as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3153748	3			0	ast	Register the new components within $[officename]. This can be accomplished using the tool \<item type=\"literal\"\>unopkg\</item\>, which can be found in {installpath}\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>/ \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>\\\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>program.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3153345				0	ast	Integrate the new components as services. The ProtocolHandler and JobDispatch services assist you; more information can be found in the $[officename] Developer's Guide.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3166460				0	ast	Change the user interface (menus or toolbars). This can be done almost automatically by writing an XML text file that describes the changes. More information can be found in the $[officename] Developer's Guide.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\integratinguno.xhp	0	help	par_id3151110				0	ast	The Add-Ons can extend the functionality of $[officename]. They are not related to the \<switchinline select=\"appl\"\>\<caseinline select=\"CALC\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"Add-Ins\"\>Add-Ins\</link\>\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Add-Ins\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> that provide new functions for $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Protecting Content in %PRODUCTNAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150620				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>protecting; contents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>protected contents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>contents protection\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>encryption of contents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>passwords for protecting contents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>security;protecting contents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>form controls; protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>draw objects;protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>OLE objects;protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>graphics;protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>frames;protecting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155364				0	ast	\<variable id=\"protection\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/protection.xhp\" name=\"Protecting Content in %PRODUCTNAME\"\>Protecting Content in \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3153394				0	ast	The following is an overview of the different ways of protecting contents in \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> from being modified, deleted or viewed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146957				0	ast	Protecting All Documents When Saving				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3150775				0	ast	All documents that are saved in \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000021.xhp\" name=\"OpenDocument format\"\>OpenDocument format\</link\> can be saved with a password. Documents that are saved with a password cannot be opened without the password. The content is secured so that it cannot be read with an external editor. This applies to content, graphics and OLE objects.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3166410				0	ast	Turning on protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3145121				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>File - Save As\</emph\> and mark the \<emph\>Save with password\</emph\> check box. Save the document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3154286				0	ast	Turning off protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3148492				0	ast	Open the document, entering the correct password. Choose \<emph\>File - Save As\</emph\> and clear the \<emph\>Save with password\</emph\> check box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3145068				0	ast	Information entered in \<emph\>File - Properties\</emph\> is not encrypted. This includes the name of the author, creation date, word and character counts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149294				0	ast	Protecting Revision Marking				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3161646				0	ast	With every change made in \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc and \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Writer, the review function records who made the change. This function can be turned on with protection, so that it can only be turned off when the correct password is entered. Until then, all changes will continue to be recorded. Acceptance or rejection of changes is not possible.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3154684				0	ast	Turning on protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3153104				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Changes - Protect Records\</emph\>. Enter and confirm a password of at least 5 characters.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3144760				0	ast	Turning off protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3152920				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Changes - Protect Records\</emph\>. Enter the correct password.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155113				0	ast	Protecting Frames, Graphics, and OLE Objects				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3153703				0	ast	You can protect the content, position and size of inserted graphics. The same applies to frames (in Writer) and OLE objects.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3147131				0	ast	Turning on protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	ast	For example, for graphics inserted in Writer: Choose \<emph\>Format - Picture - Options\</emph\> tab. Under \<emph\>Protect\</emph\>, mark \<emph\>Contents\</emph\>, \<emph\>Position\</emph\> and/or \<emph\>Size\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3147510				0	ast	Turning off protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3153657				0	ast	For example, for graphics inserted in Writer: Choose \<emph\>Format - Picture - Options\</emph\> tab. Under \<emph\>Protect\</emph\>, unmark as appropriate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152992				0	ast	Protecting Drawing Objects and Form Objects				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3166429	59			0	ast	The draw objects that you insert into your documents with the \<emph\>Drawing \</emph\>toolbar can be protected from being accidentally moved or changed in size. You can do the same with form objects inserted with the \<emph\>Form Controls\</emph\> toolbar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3153226				0	ast	Turning on protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3148815	61			0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Format - Object - Position and Size \</emph\>- \<emph\>Position and Size\</emph\> tab. Mark the \<emph\>Position\</emph\> or \<emph\>Size\</emph\> check box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3156289				0	ast	Turning off protection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id3154991	63			0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Format - Object - Position and Size \</emph\>- \<emph\>Position and Size\</emph\> tab. Unmark the \<emph\>Position\</emph\> or \<emph\>Size\</emph\> check box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B8C				0	ast	\<embedvar href=\"text/shared/guide/digital_signatures.xhp#digital_signatures\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id4680928				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/swriter/guide/protection.xhp\"\>Protecting Content in %PRODUCTNAME Writer\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\protection.xhp	0	help	par_id9014252				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_protect.xhp\"\>Protecting Cells in %PRODUCTNAME Calc\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Inserting Charts				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153910				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>charts; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>plotting data as charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; inserting charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>charts; editing data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; chart data\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153910				0	ast	\<variable id=\"chart_insert\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/chart_insert.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Charts\"\>Inserting Charts\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3139133				0	ast	Different methods exist to start a chart:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6772972				0	ast	Insert a chart based on data from cells in Calc or Writer.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6049684				0	ast	These charts update automatically when the source data changes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id2356944				0	ast	Insert a chart with a default data set, and then use the Data Table dialog to enter your own data for that chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id866115				0	ast	These charts can be created in Writer, Impress and Draw. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3146763				0	ast	Copy a chart from Calc or Writer into another document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id701315				0	ast	These charts are snapshots of the data at the time of copying. They do not change when the source data changes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id4439832				0	ast	In Calc, a chart is an object on a sheet, it cannot be a sheet of its own.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id719931				0	ast	Chart in a Calc spreadsheet				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	ast	Click inside the cell range that you want to present in your chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7211218				0	ast	Click the \<emph\>Insert Chart\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> toolbar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7549363				0	ast	You see a chart preview and the Chart Wizard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id9091769				0	ast	Follow the instructions in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> to create the chart. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3761406				0	ast	Chart in a Writer text document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3155066	32			0	ast	In a Writer document, you can insert a chart based on the values in a Writer table. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id428479				0	ast	Click inside the Writer table. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7236243				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Object - Chart\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6171452				0	ast	You see a chart preview and the Chart Wizard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3145419	7			0	ast	Follow the instructions in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> to create the chart. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id6436658				0	ast	Chart based on values of its own				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6944792				0	ast	In Writer, if you have not selected any cells, choose \<emph\>Insert - Object - Chart\</emph\> to insert a chart with default data. In Draw or Impress, choose \<emph\>Insert - Chart\</emph\> to insert a chart based on default data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	ast	You can change the default data values by double-clicking on the chart and then choosing \<link href=\"text/schart/01/03010000.xhp\" name=\"View - Chart Data Table\"\>\<emph\>View - Chart Data Table\</emph\>\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Working with Forms				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	bm_id5762199				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>opening;forms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>forms;creating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>design view;creating forms\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN105F9				0	ast	\<variable id=\"data_forms\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/data_forms.xhp\"\>Working with Forms\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10617				0	ast	Using forms, you can define how to present the data. Open a text document or a spreadsheet and insert the controls such as push buttons and list boxes. In the properties dialogs of the controls, you can define what data the forms should display.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061A				0	ast	Creating a New Form With the Form Wizard				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061E				0	ast	In %PRODUCTNAME, you can create a new form using the \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01090000.xhp\"\>Form Wizard\</link\>:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	ast	Open the database file where you want to create the new form.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10636				0	ast	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063E				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Use Wizard to Create Form\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10645				0	ast	Creating a New Form Manually				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064C				0	ast	Open the database file where you want to create the new form.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10650				0	ast	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10658				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Create Form in Design View\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065F				0	ast	A new text document opens. Use the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170000.xhp\"\>Form Controls\</link\> to insert form controls.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10670				0	ast	Click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon to access all forms that were created from within the current database window. In addition, you can use the \<emph\>Form Controls\</emph\> icons to add database form controls to any Writer or Calc document, but these documents will not be listed in the database window. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	About Digital Signatures				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	bm_id7430951				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>certificates\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>digital signatures;overview\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>security;digital signatures\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id2767418				0	ast	\<variable id=\"digital_signatures\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/digital_signatures.xhp\"\>About Digital Signatures\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	ast	In %PRODUCTNAME, you can digitally sign your documents and macros. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id6564531				0	ast	Certificates				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10639				0	ast	To sign a document digitally, you need a personal key, the certificate. A personal key is stored on your computer as a combination of a private key, which must be kept secret, and a public key, which you add to your documents when you sign them.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	ast	Save and sign the document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10671				0	ast	When you apply a digital signature to a document, a kind of checksum is computed from the document's content plus your personal key. The checksum and your public key are stored together with the document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10674				0	ast	Open a signed document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10678				0	ast	When someone later opens the document on any computer with a recent version of %PRODUCTNAME, the program will compute the checksum again and compare it with the stored checksum. If both are the same, the program will signal that you see the original, unchanged document. In addition, the program can show you the public key information from the certificate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067B				0	ast	You can compare the public key with the public key that is published on the web site of the certificate authority.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067E				0	ast	Whenever someone changes something in the document, this change breaks the digital signature. After the change, there will be no sign that you see the original document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911381426				0	ast	The result of the signature validation is displayed in the status bar and within the Digital Signature dialog. Several documents and macro signatures can exist inside an ODF document. If there is a problem with one signature, then the validation result of that one signature is assumed for all signatures. That is, if there are ten valid signatures and one invalid signature, then the status bar and the status field in the dialog will flag the signature as invalid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200911571878				0	ast	You can see any of the following icons and messages when you open a signed document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504050				0	ast	Icon in Status bar				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504061				0	ast	Signature status				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504010				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912421569\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/certificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912421569\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504189				0	ast	The signature is valid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id082120091250418				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912431081\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/notcertificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912431081\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504133				0	ast	The signature is OK, but the certificates could not be validated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504165				0	ast	The signature and the certificate are OK, but not all parts of the document are signed. (For documents that were signed with old versions of the software, see note below.)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504237				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912435090\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/caution_11x16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1146in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912435090\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504233				0	ast	The signature is invalid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id0821200910191787				0	ast	Signatures and software versions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191747				0	ast	The signing of contents got changed with OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2. Now all contents of the files, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml) are signed. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191774				0	ast	When you sign a document with OpenOffice.org 3.2 or StarOffice 9.2 or a later version, and you open that document in an older version of the software, the signature will be displayed as "invalid". Signatures created with older versions of the software will be marked with "only parts of the documents are signed" when loaded in the newer software.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911583098				0	ast	When you load an ODF document, you might see an icon in the status bar and the status field in the dialog that indicates that the document is only partially signed. This status will appear when the signature and certificate are valid, but they were created with a version of OpenOffice.org before 3.2 or StarOffice before 9.2. In versions of OpenOffice.org before 3.0 or StarOffice before 9.0, the document signature was applied to the main contents, pictures and embedded objects only and some contents, like macros, were not signed. In OpenOffice.org 3.0 and StarOffice 9.0 the document signature was applied to most content, including macros. However, the mimetype and the content of the META-INF folder were not signed. And in OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2 all contents, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml), are signed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id9354228				0	ast	Security Warnings				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2372508				0	ast	When you receive a signed document, and the software reports that the signature is valid, this does not mean that you can be absolutely sure that the document is the same that the sender has sent. Signing documents with software certificates is not a perfectly secure method. Numerous ways are possible to circumvent the security features.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id7953123				0	ast	Example: Think about someone who wants to camouflage his identity to be a sender from your bank. He can easily get a certificate using a false name, then send you any signed e-mail pretending he is working for your bank. You will get that e-mail, and the e-mail or the document within has the "valid signed" icon. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6195257				0	ast	Do not trust the icon. Inspect and verify the certificates.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id8635517				0	ast	The validation of a signature is not a legally binding guarantee of any kind.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6075624				0	ast	On Windows operating systems, the Windows features of validating a signature are used. On Solaris and Linux systems, files that are supplied by Thunderbird, Mozilla or Firefox are used. You must ensure that the files that are in use within your system are really the original files that were supplied by the original developers. For malevolent intruders, there are numerous ways to replace original files with other files that they supply.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6819971				0	ast	The messages about validation of a signature that you see in %PRODUCTNAME are the messages that the validation files return. The %PRODUCTNAME software has no way to ensure that the messages reflect the true status of any certificate. The %PRODUCTNAME software only displays the messages that other files that are not under control of %PRODUCTNAME report. There is no legal responsibility of %PRODUCTNAME that the displayed messages reflect the true status of a digital signature.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id3204443				0	ast	\<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/How_to_use_digital_Signatures\"\>English Wiki page on digital signatures\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id486465				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/digitalsign_send.xhp\"\>Applying digital signatures\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id3448591				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/digitalsign_receive.xhp\"\>Opening a document using WebDAV over HTTPS\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150713				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>Gallery; hiding/showing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data source view; showing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Navigator; docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Styles and Formatting window; docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>windows; hiding/showing/docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>docking; windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>undocking windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>showing;docked windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hiding;docked windows\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145346				0	ast	\<variable id=\"autohide\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/autohide.xhp\" name=\"Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows\"\>Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3147242				0	ast	Some windows in $[officename] are dockable, such as the Navigator window. You can move these windows, re-size them or dock them to an edge.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154750				0	ast	Docking and Undocking Windows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3166460				0	ast	To dock a window, do one of the following:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3150503				0	ast	Drag the window by its title bar to the side, or				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	ast	Double-click inside a vacant area of the window while holding down the Ctrl key. In the Styles and Formatting window, double-click a gray part of the window next to the icons while holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>key. Alternatively, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+F10\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3147335				0	ast	These methods can also be used to undock a currently docked window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149796				0	ast	Showing and Hiding Docked Windows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3149045				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id3149655\" src=\"res/helpimg/ein.png\" width=\"0.1043inch\" height=\"0.4272inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149655\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3152921	64			0	ast	Click the button on the edge of the docked window to show or hide the docked window. The AutoHide function allows you to temporarily show a hidden window by clicking on its edge. When you click in the document, the docked window hides again. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Editing Chart Legends				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147291				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>charts; editing legends\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>legends; charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; chart legends\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting; chart legends\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147291				0	ast	\<variable id=\"chart_legend\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/chart_legend.xhp\" name=\"Editing Chart Legends\"\>Editing Chart Legends\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3147008				0	ast	To edit a chart legend:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3146957				0	ast	Double-click on the chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3154824				0	ast	A gray border appears around the chart and the menu bar now contains commands for editing the objects in the chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3153031				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Format - Legend\</emph\> or double-click on the legend. This opens the \<emph\>Legend\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3147210				0	ast	Choose from the available tabs to make modifications, then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3145674				0	ast	To select the legend, first double-click on the chart (see step 1), then click on the legend. You can now move the legend within the chart using the mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_legend.xhp	0	help	par_id3154347				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/05010000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Object Properties\"\>Format - Object Properties\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Setting up Printer and Fax Under UNIX Based Platforms				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154422				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>spadmin\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printers; adding, UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>default printer; UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>standard printer under UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>faxes; fax programs/fax printers under UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printers; faxes under UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PostScript; PDF converter, UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>converters; PostScript, UNIX\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PDF; PostScript to PDF converter, UNIX\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147834				0	ast	\<variable id=\"spadmin\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/spadmin.xhp\" name=\"Setting up Printer and Fax Under UNIX Based Platforms\"\>Setting up Printer and Fax Under UNIX Based Platforms\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3159876				0	ast	%PRODUCTNAME uses the installed fonts of your system. In a text document you can select from all printable fonts. In an HTML document or in Web layout, only fonts that are visible on screen are offered. In spreadsheets and drawings you can select from all installed fonts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3159233				0	ast	Under UNIX based platforms, the printer administration program \<emph\>spadmin\</emph\> is provided to help you set up printers and faxes for use with the $[officename] software.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3156410				0	ast	Call the printer administration program \<emph\>spadmin\</emph\> as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147242				0	ast	Change to the {install_path}/program directory.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147209				0	ast	Enter: ./spadmin				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3152349				0	ast	After it starts, the window of the printer administration program \<emph\>spadmin\</emph\> appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3149580				0	ast	Following a server installation, the system administrator first logs on as with root privileges, and starts the printer administration program \<emph\>spadmin\</emph\>. The administrator then creates a general printer configuration file called {install_path}/share/psprint/psprint.conf for all users. All changes are immediately available to all users.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159177				0	ast	Setting up Printers				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3159157				0	ast	Under UNIX based platforms, the $[officename] software only offers direct support for printers using the PostScript technology. Other printers must be set up as described in the section \<emph\>Printer Drivers in the $[officename] Software\</emph\>. The $[officename] software automatically provides a printer with the default driver for each system queue. You can add additional printers as needed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148564				0	ast	Adding a Printer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147559				0	ast	Change to the {install_path}/program directory.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3152360				0	ast	Enter: ./spadmin				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3153360				0	ast	Click the \<emph\>New Printer\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	ast	Select the \<emph\>Create Printer\</emph\> option and click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3153192				0	ast	Select the appropriate driver for your printer. If you are not using a PostScript printer or your model is not listed, use the \<emph\>Generic Printer\</emph\> driver or follow the steps below. You can also add new drivers using the \<emph\>Import\</emph\> button or delete unnecessary drivers using the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> button. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3125863				0	ast	Choose a command line that you can use to print on your printer (for example, lp -d my_queue). Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	ast	Give the printer a name and determine whether it should become the default printer. Click \<emph\>Finish\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3155429				0	ast	To print a test page, click on \<emph\>Test Page\</emph\>. If the test page fails to print or is incorrectly printed, check all settings as described in \<emph\>Changing Printer Settings\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146147				0	ast	Printer Drivers in the $[officename] Software				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147428				0	ast	When installing a non-PostScript printer, you must set your system so that PostScript can be converted into the language of the printer. We recommend using current PostScript conversion software such as Ghostscript (http://www.cs.wisc.edu/~ghost/).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154639				0	ast	You should set up the \<emph\>Generic Printer\</emph\> in that case. Also make sure that the page margins are set correctly.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153				0	ast	If you are using a printer using the PostScript technology, you should install a description file for the printer (PostScript Printer Definition - PPD) so that you can utilize the specific printer features like paper tray selection, duplex print function and all built-in fonts. You can also use the generic printer driver since it contains the most important data and is suitable for most printers. In that case, you will have to do without the paper tray selection and must correctly set the page margins.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3146976	128			0	ast	Some PPD files are installed as default files. If there is no matching PPD file for your printer, you will find various PPD files at http://www.adobe.com/products/printerdrivers/. You can also ask the manufacturer of your printer for PPD files. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147435				0	ast	Importing Drivers When Creating a New Printer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Import\</emph\> in the driver selection dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260	347			0	ast	Click \<emph\>Browse\</emph\> to select the directory where you unpacked the PPD files. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147443				0	ast	In the \<emph\>Selection of drivers\</emph\> list box, select the printer driver you want to install.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	ast	Click OK.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152885				0	ast	Deleting Drivers When Creating a New Printer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729				0	ast	Select the printer driver.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3155417				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Delete\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3149909				0	ast	Be sure that you do not delete the generic printer driver, and remember that drivers deleted from server installations are no longer available to other users who are using the same server installation.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	ast	If the printer has more fonts built in than the usual PostScript fonts, you must also load the AFM files for these additional fonts. Copy the AFM files into the {install_path}/share/psprint/fontmetric directory or into the {install_path}/user/psprint/fontmetric directory. You can find AFM files, for example, at ftp://ftp.adobe.com/pub/adobe/type/win/all/afmfiles/.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148388				0	ast	Changing Printer Settings				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ast	In the printer administration program \<emph\>spadmin\</emph\>, select the printer from the \<emph\>Installed printers\</emph\> list box and click \<emph\>Properties\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> dialog appears containing several tab pages. This is where you can make settings that are used according to the PPD file of the selected printer.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3148833				0	ast	Select the command on the \<emph\>Command\</emph\> tab page. You can remove superfluous commands using the \<emph\>Remove\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154270				0	ast	On the \<emph\>Paper\</emph\> tab page, you can define the paper format and paper tray to be used as the default settings for this printer.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3145649				0	ast	On the \<emph\>Device\</emph\> tab page, you can activate the special options for your printer. If your printer can only print in black and white, choose "grayscale" under \<emph\>Color\</emph\>, otherwise choose "color". If switching to grayscale leads to unfavorable results, you can also select "color" under \<emph\>Color\</emph\> and see how the printer or PostScript emulator applies it. Furthermore, on this tab page you can set the precision with which colors are described as well as the PostScript level.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	ast	The \<emph\>Font Replacement\</emph\> tab page allows you to select a printer font type available in the printer for each font type installed on your computer. This way you can reduce the data volume sent to the printer. Font replacement can be turned on or off for each printer individually.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3153924				0	ast	You should also set the page margins correctly on the \<emph\>Additional settings\</emph\> tab when using the generic printer driver so that your printout is not cropped. You can also enter a description in the Comments field, which will also be displayed on the \<emph\>Print\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	ast	Some of these settings can also be set for the current printout in the \<emph\>Print\</emph\> dialog or the \<emph\>Printer Settings\</emph\> dialog in the $[officename] software with the \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150042				0	ast	Renaming or Deleting Printers				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147338				0	ast	Select a printer from the \<emph\>Installed printers\</emph\> list box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	ast	To rename the selected printer, click \<emph\>Rename\</emph\>. Enter an appropriate name in the dialog that appears and click OK. The name must be unique and should be chosen so that you can recognize the printer and the application. Printer names must be assigned the same for all users because, when documents are exchanged, the selected printer remains unchanged if the recipient has it under the same name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	ast	To delete the selected printer, click \<emph\>Remove\</emph\>. The default printer or a printer that has been created by the system administrator in a server installation cannot be removed using this dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147346				0	ast	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Selecting a Default Printer \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3145769				0	ast	To make the printer selected from the \<emph\>Installed printers\</emph\> list box the default printer, double-click its name or click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154204				0	ast	Using Fax Functionality				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3148463				0	ast	If you have installed a fax package such as Efax or HylaFax on your computer, you can send faxes with the $[officename] software.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154575				0	ast	Change to the {install_path}/program directory.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3151277				0	ast	Enter: ./spadmin				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3146808				0	ast	Click \<emph\>New Printer\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Add Printer\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3151070				0	ast	Select \<emph\>Connect a fax device\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150787				0	ast	Choose whether to use the default driver or another printer driver. If you are not using the default driver, select the appropriate driver. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3155111				0	ast	Enter a command line with which to communicate with the fax in the following dialog. In the command line of each fax sent "(TMP)" is replaced by a temporary file and "(PHONE)" by the telephone number of the recipient fax machine. If "(TMP)" occurs in the command line, the PostScript code is transmitted in a file, otherwise as standard input through a pipe. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3146135				0	ast	Assign a name to your new fax printer and determine whether the telephone numbers (see below) marked in the text should be removed from the printout, or not. Click \<emph\>Finish\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3147506				0	ast	You can now send faxes by printing to the printer that has just been created.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3148419				0	ast	Enter the fax number as text in the document. You can also enter a field that takes the fax number from your active database. In any case, the fax number must start with the characters @@# and end with @@. A valid entry would have the form @@#1234567@@.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150826				0	ast	If these characters including the telephone number are not printed, activate the \<emph\>Fax number is removed from the printout\</emph\> option under \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> on the \<emph\>Command\</emph\> tab page. If no telephone number is entered in the document, a dialog prompting you for it will appear after the printout.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196				0	ast	In $[officename] you can also activate an icon for sending faxes to a default fax. To do this, choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize - Toolbars\</emph\>, click \<emph\>Add Commands\</emph\> and add from "Documents" the \<emph\>Send Default Fax\</emph\> icon. You can set which fax is used when this button is pressed under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Writer - Print\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150517				0	ast	Remember to create one separate print job for each fax, otherwise, the first recipient will receive all the faxes. In the \<emph\>Tools - Mail Merge\</emph\> dialog select the \<emph\>Printer\</emph\> option and then select the \<emph\>Single print jobs\</emph\> check box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146929				0	ast	Connecting a PostScript to PDF Converter				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3154989				0	ast	If a PostScript to PDF converter such as Ghostscript or Adobe Acrobat Distiller is installed on your computer, you can quickly create PDF documents in the $[officename] software.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3150524				0	ast	In spadmin, click \<emph\>New Printer\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Add Printer\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3145268				0	ast	Select \<emph\>Connect a PDF converter\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3156445				0	ast	Choose whether to use the default driver, the Acrobat Distiller driver or another driver. The driver "Generic Printer (T42 enabled)" prefers the type 42 fonts over type 3 fonts; this driver is a good choice for output to a software PostScript interpreter. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3155128				0	ast	In the next dialog, enter a command line for the PostScript->PDF Converter. Enter the directory where the PDF files should be saved. If you do not provide a directory, the user's home directory will be used. In the command line "(TMP)" is replaced by a temporary file and "(OUTFILE)" by the target file, the name of which is created from the document name. If "(TMP)" occurs in the command line, the Postscript code is forwarded in a file, otherwise as standard input through a pipe. If Ghostscript or Adobe Acrobat Distiller is in the search path, you can use one of the predefined command lines. Click \<emph\>Next\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889				0	ast	Assign a name to your new PDF converter. Click \<emph\>Finish\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\spadmin.xhp	0	help	par_id3153064				0	ast	You can now create PDF documents by printing to the converter that has just been created.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Scripting %PRODUCTNAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	bm_id5277565				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>assigning scripts\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>programming;scripting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>form controls;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>pictures;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hyperlinks;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>shortcut keys;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>controls;assigning macros (Basic)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>menus;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>events;assigning scripts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070A				0	ast	\<variable id=\"scripting\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/scripting.xhp\"\>Assigning Scripts in %PRODUCTNAME\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10728				0	ast	You can assign custom scripts (macros) to menu items, icons, dialog controls, and events in %PRODUCTNAME.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1072B				0	ast	%PRODUCTNAME internally supports the following scripting languages:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	ast	%PRODUCTNAME Basic				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10739				0	ast	JavaScript				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073D				0	ast	BeanShell				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_id6797082				0	ast	Python				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091F				0	ast	In addition, developers can use high-level languages, for example Java programming language, to control %PRODUCTNAME externally. See the API project at Apache OpenOffice.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10751				0	ast	To assign a script to a new menu entry				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10758				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize\</emph\>, and click the \<emph\>Menus\</emph\> tab.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1093D				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10760				0	ast	In the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10768				0	ast	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1076C				0	ast	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select a script.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10770				0	ast	A list of the script functions appears in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> list box. Select a function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10778				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> to create a new menu assignment. The new menu entry appears in the \<emph\>Entries\</emph\> list box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10783				0	ast	To assign a script to a key combination				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10787				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize - Keyboard\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A59				0	ast	In the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A61				0	ast	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A65				0	ast	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A69				0	ast	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A71				0	ast	Click the option button for %PRODUCTNAME or Writer (or whichever application is currently open). 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A74				0	ast	Selecting the option button sets the scope of the new key combination to be applicable in all of %PRODUCTNAME or only in documents of the current module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A78				0	ast	Select a key combination from the \<emph\>Shortcut keys\</emph\> list box and click \<emph\>Modify\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078A				0	ast	To assign a script to an event				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078E				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize - Events\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A16				0	ast	Click \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A9E				0	ast	In the \<emph\>Library\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AA6				0	ast	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AAA				0	ast	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AAE				0	ast	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Assigned Action\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB6				0	ast	Select to save in %PRODUCTNAME or current document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB9				0	ast	This sets the scope of the new event assignment to be applicable in all of %PRODUCTNAME or only in documents of the current module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ABD				0	ast	Select an event from the list and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10791				0	ast	To assign a script to an event for an embedded object				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10795				0	ast	Select the embedded object, for example a chart, in your document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ADB				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Format - Frame/Object - Macro\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ADF				0	ast	In the \<emph\>Macros\</emph\> list box, open the %PRODUCTNAME Scripts entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AE7				0	ast	You see entries for share (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), user (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AEB				0	ast	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AEF				0	ast	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Existing macros in\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AF7				0	ast	Select an event from the list and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10798				0	ast	To assign a script to a hyperlink				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079C				0	ast	Position the cursor inside the hyperlink. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B15				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Hyperlink\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B19				0	ast	Click the \<emph\>Events\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B21				0	ast	Select and assign as stated above.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079F				0	ast	To assign a script to a graphic				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A3				0	ast	Select the graphic in your document. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B3B				0	ast	Choose \<emph\>Format - Picture - Macro\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B3F				0	ast	Select and assign as stated above.				20200411 15:09:53
