helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153493				0	ast	Boolean NOT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153505				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_NEGX\"\>Inserts a \<emph\>Boolean NOT\</emph\> with one placeholder.\</ahelp\> You can also type \<emph\>neg<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10383				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id3147116\" src=\"starmath/res/un21214.png\" width=\"0.3335inch\" height=\"0.3335inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147116\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149847				0	ast	Boolean AND				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147599				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_XANDY\"\>Inserts a \<emph\>Boolean AND\</emph\> with two placeholders.\</ahelp\> You can also type \<emph\><?>and<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_idN103C3				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id3148440\" src=\"starmath/res/un21215.png\" width=\"0.3335inch\" height=\"0.3335inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3148440\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151086				0	ast	Boolean OR				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154076				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_XORY\"\>Inserts a \<emph\>Boolean OR\</emph\> with two placeholders.\</ahelp\> You can also type \<emph\><?>or<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10403				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id3150173\" src=\"starmath/res/un21221.png\" width=\"0.3335inch\" height=\"0.3335inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150173\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151129				0	ast	Concatenate				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156102	39			0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_XCIRCY\"\>Inserts a \<emph\>concatenation sign\</emph\> with two placeholders. \</ahelp\> You can also type \<emph\>circ\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150464				0	ast	You can also insert user-defined unary operators by typing \<emph\>uoper\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, followed by the syntax for the character. This function is useful for incorporating special characters into a formula. For example, the command \<emph\>uoper %theta x\</emph\> produces a small Greek letter theta (a component of the \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> character set). You can also insert characters not in the $[officename] character set by choosing \<emph\>Tools - Catalog - Edit\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154725				0	ast	 You can also insert user-defined binary commands by typing \<emph\>boper\</emph\> into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window. For example, the command \<emph\>y boper %theta x\</emph\> produces the small Greek letter theta preceded by a \<emph\>y\</emph\> and followed by an \<emph\>x\</emph\>. You can also insert characters not in the $[officename] character set by choosing \<emph\>Tools - Catalog - Edit\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150906				0	ast	By typing \<emph\><?>oplus<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, you insert a \<emph\>circled plus operator\</emph\> in your document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151197				0	ast	Type \<emph\><?>ominus<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to insert a \<emph\>circled minus operator\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155082				0	ast	Type \<emph\><?>odot<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to insert a \<emph\>circled dot operator\</emph\> in the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154331				0	ast	Type \<emph\><?>odivide<?>\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to insert a \<emph\>circled division operator\</emph\> in the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150089				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_XWIDESLASHY\"\>Type \<emph\>a wideslash b\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to produce two characters with a slash (from lower left to upper right) between them.\</ahelp\> The characters are set such that everything to the left of the slash is up, and everything to the right is down. This command is also available in the context menu of the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150024				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_XWIDEBSLASHY\"\>Type \<emph\>a widebslash b\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to produce two characters with a slash (from upper left to lower right) between them.\</ahelp\> The characters are set such that everything to the left of the slash is down, and everything to the right is up. This command is also available in the context menu of the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149376				0	ast	Type \<emph\>sub\</emph\> or \<emph\>sup\</emph\> in the Commands window to add indexes and powers to the characters in your formula; for example, a sub 2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155383				0	ast	If you want to use a colon ':' as division sign, choose \<emph\>Tools - Catalog\</emph\> or click the \<emph\>Catalog\</emph\> icon on the Tools bar. Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button in the dialog that appears, then select the \<emph\>Special\</emph\> symbol set. Enter a meaningful name next to \<emph\>Symbol\</emph\>, for example, "divide" and then click the colon in the set of symbols. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> and then \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to close the \<emph\>Symbols\</emph\> dialog,too. Now you can use the new symbol, in this case the colon, by entering its name in the Commands window, for example, \<emph\>a %divide b = c\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147398				0	ast	When entering information manually in the Commands window, please note that a number of operators require spaces between the elements for the correct structure. This is especially true if you are using values instead of placeholders in your operators, for example, to construct a division 4 div 3 or a div b.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090903.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Symbols with Indices				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090903.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155959				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090903.xhp\" name=\"Symbols with Indices\"\>Symbols with Indices\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090903.xhp	0	help	par_id3150300				0	ast	A third example of how to use \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> to create symbols with indexes is shown below. You can copy the example into the clipboard and use it in your own formula in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090903.xhp	0	help	par_id3148866				0	ast	\<image id=\"img_id3153246\" src=\"res/helpimg/smzb3.png\" width=\"5.673cm\" height=\"2.997cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3153246\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090903.xhp	0	help	par_id3153808				0	ast	%PHI^{i_1 i_2 dotsaxis i_n}_{k_1 k_2 dotsaxis k_n}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Font Sizes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153816				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>font sizes; in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sizes; of fonts in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153816				0	ast	Font Sizes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	ast	\<variable id=\"schriftgroessentext\"\>\<ahelp hid=\"SID_FONTSIZE\"\>Use this dialog to specify the font sizes for your formula. Select a base size and all elements of the formula will be scaled in relation to this base.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146968				0	ast	Base size				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145115				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"STARMATH:METRICFIELD:RID_FONTSIZEDIALOG:1\"\>All elements of a formula are proportionally scaled to the base size. To change the base size, select or type in the desired point (pt) size. You can also use other units of measure or other \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#metrik\" name=\"metrics\"\>metrics\</link\>, which are then automatically converted to points.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153005				0	ast	To permanently change the default size (12 pt) used in $[officename] Math, you must first set the size (for example, 11 pt) and then click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153735				0	ast	Relative Sizes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241				0	ast	In this section, you can determine the relative sizes for each type of element with reference to the base size.				20200411 15:09:53
