helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149756				0	ast	DISC("Settlement"; "Maturity"; Price; Redemption; Basis)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156014	383			0	ast	            \<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	384			0	ast	            \<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159180	385			0	ast	            \<emph\>Price\</emph\> is the price of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147253	386			0	ast	            \<emph\>Redemption\</emph\> is the redemption value of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151174				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155902				0	ast	A security is purchased on 2001-01-25; the maturity date is 2001-11-15. The price (purchase price) is 97, the redemption value is 100. Using daily balance calculation (basis 3) how high is the settlement (discount)?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152797	389			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DISC("2001-01-25";"2001-11-15";97;100;3)\</item\> returns about 0.0372 or 3.72 per cent.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154695				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>DURATION_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>Microsoft Excel functions\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>durations;fixed interest securities\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154695				0	ast	DURATION_ADD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145768				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DURATION\"\>Calculates the duration of a fixed interest security in years.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153904				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153373				0	ast	DURATION_ADD("Settlement"; "Maturity"; Coupon; Yield; Frequency; Basis)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155397	406			0	ast	            \<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148558	407			0	ast	            \<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153096	408			0	ast	            \<emph\>Coupon\</emph\> is the annual coupon interest rate (nominal rate of interest)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154594	409			0	ast	            \<emph\>Yield\</emph\> is the annual yield of the security.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906	410			0	ast	            \<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146995				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148834				0	ast	A security is purchased on 2001-01-01; the maturity date is 2006-01-01. The Coupon rate of interest is 8%. The yield is 9.0%. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) how long is the duration?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154902	413			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DURATION_ADD("2001-01-01";"2006-01-01";0.08;0.09;2;3)\</item\>         				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159147				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>annual net interest rates\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>calculating; annual net interest rates\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>net annual interest rates\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>EFFECTIVE function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159147				0	ast	EFFECTIVE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154204				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EFFEKTIV\"\>Returns the net annual interest rate for a nominal interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145417				0	ast	Nominal interest refers to the amount of interest due at the end of a calculation period. Effective interest increases with the number of payments made. In other words, interest is often paid in installments (for example, monthly or quarterly) before the end of the calculation period.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150510				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148805				0	ast	EFFECTIVE(Nom; P)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149768	93			0	ast	            \<emph\>Nom\</emph\> is the nominal interest.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149334	94			0	ast	            \<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154223				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144499				0	ast	If the annual nominal interest rate is 9.75% and four interest calculation periods are defined, what is the actual interest rate (effective rate)?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150772	97			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EFFECTIVE(9.75%;4)\</item\> = 10.11% The annual effective rate is therefore 10.11%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147241				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>effective interest rates\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>EFFECT_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147241				0	ast	EFFECT_ADD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147524				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_EFFECT\"\>Calculates the effective annual rate of interest on the basis of the nominal interest rate and the number of interest payments per annum.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155364				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155118				0	ast	EFFECT_ADD(NominalRate; NPerY)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148907	418			0	ast	            \<emph\>NominalRate\</emph\> is the annual nominal rate of interest.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154274	419			0	ast	            \<emph\>NPerY \</emph\>is the number of interest payments per year.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149156				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3158426				0	ast	What is the effective annual rate of interest for a 5.25% nominal rate and quarterly payment.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148927	422			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EFFECT_ADD(0.0525;4)\</item\> returns 0.053543 or 5.3543%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149998				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; arithmetic-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>arithmetic-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>depreciations;arithmetic-degressive\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>DDB function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149998				0	ast	DDB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159190				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the arithmetic-declining method.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152361				0	ast	Use this form of depreciation if you require a higher initial depreciation value as opposed to linear depreciation. The depreciation value gets less with each period and is usually used for assets whose value loss is higher shortly after purchase (for example, vehicles, computers). Please note that the book value will never reach zero under this calculation type.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156038				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3166452				0	ast	DDB(Cost; Salvage; Life; Period; Factor)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153237	104			0	ast	            \<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787	105			0	ast	            \<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> fixes the value of an asset at the end of its life.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945	106			0	ast	            \<emph\>Life\</emph\> is the number of periods (for example, years or months) defining how long the asset is to be used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149736	107			0	ast	            \<emph\>Period\</emph\> states the period for which the value is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150243	108			0	ast	            \<emph\>Factor\</emph\> (optional) is the factor by which depreciation decreases. If a value is not entered, the default is factor 2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159274				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152882				0	ast	A computer system with an initial cost of 75,000 currency units is to be depreciated monthly over 5 years. The value at the end of the depreciation is to be 1 currency unit. The factor is 2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154106	111			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DDB(75000;1;60;12;2) \</item\>= 1,721.81 currency units. Therefore, the double-declining depreciation in the twelfth month after purchase is 1,721.81 currency units.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149962				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>depreciations;geometric-degressive\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>DB function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149962				0	ast	DB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148989				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA2\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the double-declining balance method.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156213				0	ast	This form of depreciation is used if you want to get a higher depreciation value at the beginning of the depreciation (as opposed to linear depreciation). The depreciation value is reduced with every depreciation period by the depreciation already deducted from the initial cost.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149807				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153349				0	ast	DB(Cost; Salvage; Life; Period; Month)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148462	118			0	ast	            \<emph\>Cost\</emph\> is the initial cost of an asset.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148658	119			0	ast	            \<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145371	120			0	ast	            \<emph\>Life\</emph\> defines the period over which an asset is depreciated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154608	121			0	ast	            \<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the length of each period. The length must be entered in the same date unit as the depreciation period.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150829	122			0	ast	            \<emph\>Month\</emph\> (optional) denotes the number of months for the first year of depreciation. If an entry is not defined, 12 is used as the default.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151130				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156147				0	ast	A computer system with an initial cost of 25,000 currency units is to be depreciated over a three year period. The salvage value is to be 1,000 currency units. One period is 30 days.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149513	125			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DB(25000;1000;36;1;6)\</item\> = 1,075.00 currency units				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159242				0	ast	The fixed-declining depreciation of the computer system is 1,075.00 currency units.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153948				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>IRR function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>calculating;internal rates of return, regular payments\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>internal rates of return;regular payments\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153948				0	ast	IRR				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3143282				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_IKV\"\>Calculates the internal rate of return for an investment.\</ahelp\> The values represent cash flow values at regular intervals, at least one value must be negative (payments), and at least one value must be positive (income).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150599				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155427				0	ast	IRR(Values; Guess)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144758	132			0	ast	            \<emph\>Values\</emph\> represents an array containing the values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233	133			0	ast	            \<emph\>Guess\</emph\> (optional) is the estimated value. An iterative method is used to calculate the internal rate of return. If you can provide only few values, you should provide an initial guess to enable the iteration.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151258				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630				0	ast	Under the assumption that cell contents are A1=\<item type=\"input\"\>-10000\</item\>, A2=\<item type=\"input\"\>3500\</item\>, A3=\<item type=\"input\"\>7600\</item\> and A4=\<item type=\"input\"\>1000\</item\>, the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>=IRR(A1:A4)\</item\> gives a result of 11,33%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151012				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>ISPMT function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151012				0	ast	ISPMT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148693				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ISPMT\"\>Calculates the level of interest for unchanged amortization installments.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154661				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146070				0	ast	ISPMT(Rate; Period; TotalPeriods; Invest)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148672	318			0	ast	            \<emph\>Rate\</emph\> sets the periodic interest rate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777	319			0	ast	            \<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the number of installments for calculation of interest.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153678	320			0	ast	            \<emph\>TotalPeriods\</emph\> is the total number of installment periods.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390	321			0	ast	            \<emph\>Invest\</emph\> is the amount of the investment.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149558				0	ast	For a credit amount of 120,000 currency units with a two-year term and monthly installments, at a yearly interest rate of 12% the level of interest after 1.5 years is required.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949	324			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=ISPMT(1%;18;24;120000)\</item\> = -300 currency units. The monthly interest after 1.5 years amounts to 300 currency units.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146812				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060119.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Two\"\>Financial Functions Part Two\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154411				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060118.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\"\>Financial Functions Part Three\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Define Names				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156330				0	ast	Define Names				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	ast	\<variable id=\"namenfestlegentext\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".uno:DefineName\"\>Opens a dialog where you can specify a name for a selected area.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123	31			0	ast	Use the mouse to define ranges or type the reference into the \<emph\>Define Name \</emph\>dialog fields.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	ast	The \<emph\>Sheet Area\</emph\> box on the Formula bar contains a list of defined names for the ranges. Click a name from this box to highlight the corresponding reference on the spreadsheet. Names given formulas or parts of a formula are not listed here.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151118				0	ast	Name				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163712				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:COMBOBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:ED_NAME\"\>Enter the name of the area for which you want to define a reference. All area names already defined in the spreadsheet are listed in the text field below.\</ahelp\> If you click a name on the list, the corresponding reference in the document will be shown with a blue frame. If multiple cell ranges belong to the same area name, they are displayed with different colored frames.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153728				0	ast	Assigned to				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147435				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:EDIT:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:ED_ASSIGN\"\>The reference of the selected area name is shown here as an absolute value.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	ast	To insert a new area reference, place the cursor in this field and use your mouse to select the desired area in any sheet of your spreadsheet document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154729				0	ast	More				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958	14			0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:MOREBUTTON:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_MORE\"\>Allows you to specify the \<emph\>Area type \</emph\>(optional) for the reference.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147394				0	ast	Area type				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155416				0	ast	Defines additional options related to the type of reference area.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150716				0	ast	Print range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150751				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_PRINTAREA\"\>Defines the area as a print range.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153764				0	ast	Filter				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_CRITERIA\"\>Defines the selected area to be used in an \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12040300.xhp\" name=\"advanced filter\"\>advanced filter\</link\>.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159267				0	ast	Repeat column				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_COLHEADER\"\>Defines the area as a repeating column.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153966				0	ast	Repeat row				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150300				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_ROWHEADER\"\>Defines the area as a repeating row.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155112				0	ast	Add/Modify				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159236				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:PUSHBUTTON:RID_SCDLG_NAMES:BTN_ADD\"\>Click the \<emph\>Add\</emph\> button to add the defined name to the list. Click the \<emph\>Modify\</emph\> button to enter another name for an already existing name selected from the list.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Statistical Functions Part One				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146320				0	ast	\<variable id=\"ae\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060181.xhp\"\>Statistical Functions Part One\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145632				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>INTERCEPT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>points of intersection\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>intersections\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145632				0	ast	INTERCEPT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146887				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ACHSENABSCHNITT\"\>Calculates the point at which a line will intersect the y-values by using known x-values and y-values.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150374				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149718				0	ast	INTERCEPT(DataY; DataX)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947	6			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is the dependent set of observations or data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147412	7			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is the independent set of observations or data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152983				0	ast	Names, arrays or references containing numbers must be used here. Numbers can also be entered directly.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157906				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148728				0	ast	To calculate the intercept, use cells D3:D9 as the y value and C3:C9 as the x value from the example spreadsheet. Input will be as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149013	11			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=INTERCEPT(D3:D9;C3:C9)\</item\> = 2.15.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148437				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>COUNT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>numbers;counting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148437				0	ast	COUNT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150700				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL\"\>Counts how many numbers are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are ignored.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153930				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148585				0	ast	COUNT(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155827	17			0	ast	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 values or ranges representing the values to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149254				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149953				0	ast	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154558	20			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNT(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 3. The count of numbers is therefore 3.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149729				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>COUNTA function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>number of entries\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149729				0	ast	COUNTA				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150142				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL2\"\>Counts how many values are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are also counted, even when they contain an empty string of length 0. If an argument is an array or reference, empty cells within the array or reference are ignored.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148573				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153111				0	ast	COUNTA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150001	26			0	ast	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 arguments representing the values to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150334				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154508				0	ast	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158000	29			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNTA(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 4. The count of values is therefore 4.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150267				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>B function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>probabilities of samples with binomial distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150267				0	ast	B				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156061				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_B\"\>Returns the probability of a sample with binomial distribution.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150659				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148392				0	ast	B(Trials; SP; T1; T2)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149002	35			0	ast	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148875	36			0	ast	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	37			0	ast	            \<emph\>T1\</emph\> defines the lower limit for the number of trials.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	38			0	ast	            \<emph\>T2\</emph\> (optional) defines the upper limit for the number of trials.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154633				0	ast	What is the probability with ten throws of the dice, that a six will come up exactly twice? The probability of a six (or any other number) is 1/6. The following formula combines these factors:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149393	41			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=B(10;1/6;2)\</item\> returns a probability of 29%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158416				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>determination coefficients\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>regression analysis\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158416				0	ast	RSQ				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154949				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BESTIMMTHEITSMASS\"\>Returns the square of the Pearson correlation coefficient based on the given values.\</ahelp\> RSQ (also called determination coefficient) is a measure for the accuracy of an adjustment and can be used to produce a regression analysis.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152820				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155822				0	ast	RSQ(DataY; DataX)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150470	47			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153181	48			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156258				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155991	50			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=RSQ(A1:A20;B1:B20)\</item\> calculates the determination coefficient for both data sets in columns A and B.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145620				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>BETAINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;inverse of\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145620				0	ast	BETAINV				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the cumulative beta probability density function.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152479				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156300				0	ast	BETAINV(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149266	56			0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149710	57			0	ast	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156306	58			0	ast	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150960	59			0	ast	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151268	60			0	ast	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN109DF				0	ast	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146859	62			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETAINV(0.5;5;10)\</item\> returns the value 0.33.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156096				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>BETADIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156096				0	ast	BETADIST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150880				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAVERT\"\>Returns the beta function.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150762				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571				0	ast	BETADIST(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End; Cumulative)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	68			0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156107	69			0	ast	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153619	70			0	ast	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150254	71			0	ast	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138	72			0	ast	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id012020091254453				0	ast	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional) can be 0 or False to calculate the probability density function. It can be any other value or True or omitted to calculate the cumulative distribution function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB3				0	ast	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145649				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156118	74			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETADIST(0.75;3;4)\</item\> returns the value 0.96				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143228				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>BINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228				0	ast	BINOMDIST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146897				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BINOMVERT\"\>Returns the individual term binomial distribution probability.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149289				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156009				0	ast	BINOMDIST(X; Trials; SP; C)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	80			0	ast	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of successes in a set of trials.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147492	81			0	ast	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146085	82			0	ast	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149760	83			0	ast	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the probability of a single event and \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the cumulative probability.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151171				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145666	85			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;0)\</item\> shows (if the values \<item type=\"input\"\>0\</item\> to \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\> are entered in A1) the probabilities for 12 flips of a coin that \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> will come up exactly the number of times entered in A1.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150120	86			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;1)\</item\> shows the cumulative probabilities for the same series. For example, if A1 = \<item type=\"input\"\>4\</item\>, the cumulative probability of the series is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 times \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> (non-exclusive OR).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902432928				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902421451				0	ast	CHISQINV				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902421449				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the inverse of CHISQDIST.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902475241				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475286				0	ast	            \<emph\>Probability\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse of the chi-square distribution is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475282				0	ast	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148835				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148835				0	ast	CHIINV				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the one-tailed probability of the chi-squared distribution.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159157				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504				0	ast	CHIINV(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898	92			0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the error probability.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	93			0	ast	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154208				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150777				0	ast	A die is thrown 1020 times. The numbers on the die 1 through 6 come up 195, 151, 148, 189, 183 and 154 times (observation values). The hypothesis that the die is not fixed is to be tested.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153062				0	ast	The Chi square distribution of the random sample is determined by the formula given above. Since the expected value for a given number on the die for n throws is n times 1/6, thus 1020/6 = 170, the formula returns a Chi square value of 13.27.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	ast	If the (observed) Chi square is greater than or equal to the (theoretical) Chi square CHIINV, the hypothesis will be discarded, since the deviation between theory and experiment is too great. If the observed Chi square is less that CHIINV, the hypothesis is confirmed with the indicated probability of error.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763	95			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.05;5)\</item\> returns 11.07.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159142	133			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.02;5)\</item\> returns 13.39.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158401				0	ast	If the probability of error is 5%, the die is not true. If the probability of error is 2%, there is no reason to believe it is fixed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154260				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CHITEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154260				0	ast	CHITEST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHITEST\"\>Returns the probability of a deviance from a random distribution of two test series based on the chi-squared test for independence.\</ahelp\> CHITEST returns the chi-squared distribution of the data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925				0	ast	The probability determined by CHITEST can also be determined with CHIDIST, in which case the Chi square of the random sample must then be passed as a parameter instead of the data row.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154280				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149162				0	ast	CHITEST(DataB; DataE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158421	101			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataB\</emph\> is the array of the observations.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453	102			0	ast	            \<emph\>DataE\</emph\> is the range of the expected values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146946				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096				0	ast	Data_B (observed)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948				0	ast	Data_E (expected)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152876				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159279	139			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>195\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149105	140			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148621	142			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>151\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148987	143			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149417				0	ast	3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148661	145			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>148\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151128	146			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148467				0	ast	4				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149237	148			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>189\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145304	149			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149927				0	ast	5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630	151			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>183\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150423	152			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3143275				0	ast	6				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3144750	154			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>154\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153947	155			0	ast	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	104			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHITEST(A1:A6;B1:B6)\</item\> equals 0.02. This is the probability which suffices the observed data of the theoretical Chi-square distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148690				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CHIDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148690				0	ast	CHIDIST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156338				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIVERT\"\>Returns the probability value from the indicated Chi square that a hypothesis is confirmed.\</ahelp\> CHIDIST compares the Chi square value to be given for a random sample that is calculated from the sum of (observed value-expected value)^2/expected value for all values with the theoretical Chi square distribution and determines from this the probability of error for the hypothesis to be tested.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151316				0	ast	The probability determined by CHIDIST can also be determined by CHITEST.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158439				0	ast	CHIDIST(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148675	110			0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the chi-square value of the random sample used to determine the error probability.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155615	111			0	ast	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> are the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146787				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145774	113			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIDIST(13.27; 5)\</item\> equals 0.02.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156141				0	ast	If the Chi square value of the random sample is 13.27 and if the experiment has 5 degrees of freedom, then the hypothesis is assured with a probability of error of 2%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902231887				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>chi-square distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200901583452				0	ast	CHISQDIST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200901583471				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the value of the probability density function or the cumulative distribution function for the chi-square distribution.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902395520				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395679				0	ast	CHISQDIST(Number; Degrees Of Freedom; Cumulative)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id011920090239564				0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the number for which the function is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395660				0	ast	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395623				0	ast	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional): 0 or False calculates the probability density function. Other values or True or omitted calculates the cumulative distribution function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150603				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150603				0	ast	EXPONDIST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149563				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EXPONVERT\"\>Returns the exponential distribution.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153789				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150987				0	ast	EXPONDIST(Number; Lambda; C)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154663	119			0	ast	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154569	120			0	ast	            \<emph\>Lambda\</emph\> is the parameter value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	121			0	ast	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> is a logical value that determines the form of the function. \<emph\>C = 0\</emph\> calculates the density function, and \<emph\>C = 1\</emph\> calculates the distribution.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146133				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150357	123			0	ast	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EXPONDIST(3;0.5;1)\</item\> returns 0.78.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	AutoInput				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	bm_id2486037				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>entering entries with AutoInput function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>capital letters;AutoInput function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148492				0	ast	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06130000.xhp\" name=\"AutoInput\"\>AutoInput\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150793				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:AutoComplete\"\>Switches the AutoInput function on and off, which automatically completes entries, based on other entries in the same column.\</ahelp\> The column is scanned up to a maximum of 2000 cells or 200 different strings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422				0	ast	The completion text is highlighted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	ast	To accept the completion, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> or a cursor key.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN10665				0	ast	To append text or to edit the completion, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F2\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	ast	To view more completions, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> to scroll forward, or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift+Tab\</item\> to scroll backward.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN10679				0	ast	To see a list of all available AutoInput text items for the current column, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+D.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439				0	ast	When typing formulas using characters that match previous entries, a Help tip will appear listing the last ten functions used from \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, from all defined range names, from all database range names, and from the content of all label ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	ast	AutoInput is case-sensitive. If, for example, you have written "Total" in a cell, you cannot enter "total" in another cell of the same column without first deactivating AutoInput.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ast	Array Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147273				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>matrices; functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Function Wizard; arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>array formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inline array constants\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;array functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; array formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; array formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>adjusting array ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating;conditional calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>matrices; calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>conditional calculations with arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>implicit array handling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>forced array handling\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147273				0	ast	Array Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154744				0	ast	\<variable id=\"matrixtext\"\>This category contains the array functions. \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146084				0	ast	What is an Array?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154298				0	ast	\<variable id=\"wasmatrix\"\>An array is a linked range of cells on a spreadsheet containing values. \</variable\> A square range of 3 rows and 3 columns is a 3 x 3 array:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154692				0	ast	A				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150117				0	ast	B				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155325				0	ast	C				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153104				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146996				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>7\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150529				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>31\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148831				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>33\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148943				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149771				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>95\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>17\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>2\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154904				0	ast	3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150779				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>5\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148449				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>10\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3147238				0	ast	\<item type=\"input\"\>50\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153583				0	ast	The smallest possible array is a 1 x 2 or 2 x 1 array with two adjacent cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148474				0	ast	What is an array formula?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155355				0	ast	A formula in which the individual values in a cell range are evaluated is referred to as an array formula. The difference between an array formula and other formulas is that the array formula deals with several values simultaneously instead of just one.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	ast	Not only can an array formula process several values, but it can also return several values. The results of an array formula is also an array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158432				0	ast	To multiply the values in the individual cells by 10 in the above array, you do not need to apply a formula to each individual cell or value. Instead you just need to use a single array formula. Select a range of 3 x 3 cells on another part of the spreadsheet, enter the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>=10*A1:C3\</item\> and confirm this entry using the key combination \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter. The result is a 3 x 3 array in which the individual values in the cell range (A1:C3) are multiplied by a factor of 10.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149156				0	ast	In addition to multiplication, you can also use other operators on the reference range (an array). With $[officename] Calc, you can add (+), subtract (-), multiply (*), divide (/), use exponents (^), concatenation (&) and comparisons (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=). The operators can be used on each individual value in the cell range and return the result as an array if the array formula was entered.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166456				0	ast	Comparison operators in an array formula treat empty cells in the same way as in a normal formula, that is, either as zero or as an empty string. For example, if cells A1 and A2 are empty the array formulas \<item type=\"input\"\>{=A1:A2=""}\</item\> and \<item type=\"input\"\>{=A1:A2=0}\</item\> will both return a 1 column 2 row array of cells containing TRUE.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150713				0	ast	When do you use array formulas?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787				0	ast	Use array formulas if you have to repeat calculations using different values. If you decide to change the calculation method later, you only have to update the array formula. To add an array formula, select the entire array range and then \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp\" name=\"make the required change to the array formula\"\>make the required change to the array formula\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149798				0	ast	Array formulas are also a space saving option when several values must be calculated, since they are not very memory-intensive. In addition, arrays are an essential tool for carrying out complex calculations, because you can have several cell ranges included in your calculations. $[officename] has different math functions for arrays, such as the MMULT function for multiplying two arrays or the SUMPRODUCT function for calculating the scalar products of two arrays.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155588				0	ast	Using Array Formulas in $[officename] Calc				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152876				0	ast	You can also create a "normal" formula in which the reference range, such as parameters, indicate an array formula. The result is obtained from the intersection of the reference range and the rows or columns in which the formula is found. If there is no intersection or if the range at the intersection covers several rows or columns, a #VALUE! error message appears. The following example illustrates this concept:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151271				0	ast	Creating Array Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149102				0	ast	If you create an array formula using the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, you must mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box each time so that the results are returned in an array. Otherwise, only the value in the upper-left cell of the array being calculated is returned.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153392				0	ast	If you enter the array formula directly into the cell, you must use the key combination Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter instead of the Enter key. Only then does the formula become an array formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120				0	ast	Array formulas appear in braces in $[officename] Calc. You cannot create array formulas by manually entering the braces.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154342				0	ast	The cells in a results array are automatically protected against changes. However, you can edit or copy the array formula by selecting the entire array cell range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id8834803				0	ast	Using Inline Array Constants in Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id985747				0	ast	Calc supports inline matrix/array constants in formulas. An inline array is surrounded by curly braces '{' and '}'. Elements can be each a number (including negatives), a logical constant (TRUE, FALSE), or a literal string. Non-constant expressions are not allowed. Arrays can be entered with one or more rows, and one or more columns. All rows must consist of the same number of elements, all columns must consist of the same number of elements. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id936613				0	ast	The column separator (separating elements in one row) is the ';' semicolon. The row separator is a '|' pipe symbol. The separators are not language and locale dependent. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id1877498				0	ast	Arrays can not be nested.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id4262520				0	ast	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id9387493				0	ast	={1;2;3}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8207037				0	ast	An array with one row consisting of the three numbers 1, 2, and 3.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6757103				0	ast	To enter this array constant, you select three cells in a row, then you type the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>={1;2;3}\</item\> using the curly braces and the semicolons, then press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8868068				0	ast	={1;2;3|4;5;6}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6626483				0	ast	An array with two rows and three values in each row.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id5262916				0	ast	={0;1;2|FALSE;TRUE;"two"}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id1623889				0	ast	A mixed data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id7781914				0	ast	=SIN({1;2;3})				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id300912				0	ast	Entered as a matrix formula, delivers the result of three SIN calculations with the arguments 1, 2, and 3.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148660				0	ast	Editing Array Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149241				0	ast	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula. To select the whole array, position the cell cursor inside the array range, then press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+ /, where / is the Division key on the numeric keypad.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3143274				0	ast	Either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line. Both of these actions let you edit the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154798				0	ast	After you have made changes, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150628				0	ast	You can format the separate parts of an array. For example, you can change the font color. Select a cell range and then change the attribute you want.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145608				0	ast	Copying Array Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149585				0	ast	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154619				0	ast	Either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	ast	Copy the formula into the input line by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146787				0	ast	Select a range of cells where you want to insert the array formula and either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154419				0	ast	Paste the formula by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V in the selected space and confirm it by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter. The selected range now contains the array formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154834				0	ast	Adjusting an Array Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148679				0	ast	If you want to edit the output array, do the following:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151102				0	ast	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3147096				0	ast	Below the selection, to the right, you will see a small icon with which you can zoom in or out on the range using your mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150974				0	ast	When you adjust the array range, the array formula will not automatically be adjusted. You are only changing the range in which the result will appear.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146080				0	ast	By holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>key, you can create a copy of the array formula in the given range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D47				0	ast	Conditional Array Calculations				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4B				0	ast	A conditional array calculation is an array or matrix formula that includes an IF() or CHOOSE() function. The condition argument in the formula is an area reference or a matrix result.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4E				0	ast	In the following example, the >0 test of the {=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")} formula is applied to each cell in the range A1:A3 and the result is copied to the corresponding cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D65				0	ast	A				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D6B				0	ast	B (formula)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B75				0	ast	B (result)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D79				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D80				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D86				0	ast	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D8C				0	ast	yes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D94				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D9B				0	ast	0				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DA1				0	ast	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DA7				0	ast	no				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DAF				0	ast	3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DB6				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DBC				0	ast	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DC2				0	ast	yes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DD0				0	ast	The following functions provide forced array handling: CORREL, COVAR, FORECAST, FTEST, INTERCEPT, MDETERM, MINVERSE, MMULT, MODE, PEARSON, PROB, RSQ, SLOPE, STEYX, SUMPRODUCT, SUMX2MY2, SUMX2PY2, SUMXMY2, TTEST. If you use area references as arguments when you call one of these functions, the functions behave as array functions. The following table provides an example of forced array handling:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DE2				0	ast	A				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DE8				0	ast	B (formula)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DEE				0	ast	B (result)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DF4				0	ast	C (forced array formula)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DFA				0	ast	C (result)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E02				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E09				0	ast	1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E0F				0	ast	=A1:A2+1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E17				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E1D				0	ast	=SUMPRODUCT(A1:A2+1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E25				0	ast	5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E2D				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E34				0	ast	2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E3A				0	ast	=A1:A2+1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E42				0	ast	3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E48				0	ast	=SUMPRODUCT(A1:A2+1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E50				0	ast	5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E58				0	ast	3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E63				0	ast	=A1:A2+1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E6A				0	ast	#VALUE!				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E70				0	ast	=SUMPRODUCT(A1:A2+1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E78				0	ast	5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158446				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>MUNIT function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158446				0	ast	MUNIT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154121				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EINHEITSMATRIX\"\>Returns the unitary square array of a certain size.\</ahelp\> The unitary array is a square array where the main diagonal elements equal 1 and all other array elements are equal to 0.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156271				0	ast	MUNIT(Dimensions)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390				0	ast	\<emph\>Dimensions\</emph\> refers to the size of the array unit.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10C9B				0	ast	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949				0	ast	Select a square range within the spreadsheet, for example, from A1 to E5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151260				0	ast	Without deselecting the range, select the MUNIT function. Mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. Enter the desired dimensions for the array unit, in this case \<item type=\"input\"\>5\</item\>, and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150403				0	ast	You can also enter the \<item type=\"input\"\>=Munit(5)\</item\> formula in the last cell of the selected range (E5), and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156143				0	ast	You now see a unit array with a range of A1:E5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10FA7				0	ast	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159084				0	ast	\<bookmark_value\>FREQUENCY function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159084				0	ast	FREQUENCY				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777				0	ast	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HAEUFIGKEIT\"\>Indicates the frequency distribution in a one-column-array.\</ahelp\> The function counts the number of values in the Data array that are within the values given by the Classes array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153347				0	ast	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155498				0	ast	FREQUENCY(Data; Classes)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154352				0	ast	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the reference to the values to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148402				0	ast	\<emph\>Classes\</emph\> represents the array of the limit values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D71				0	ast	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148981				0	ast	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904				0	ast	In the following table, column A lists unsorted measurement values. Column B contains the upper limit you entered for the classes into which you want to divide the data in column A. According to the limit entered in B1, the FREQUENCY function returns the number of measured values less than or equal to 5. As the limit in B2 is 10, the FREQUENCY function returns the second result as the number of measured values that are greater than 5 and less than or equal to 10. The text you entered in B6, ">25", is only for reference purposes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155869				0	ast	\<emph\>A\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149328				0	ast	\<emph\>B\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152467				0	ast	\<emph\>C\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
